ophysical Journal Letters","volume":"949"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85160286860"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-4cad16f0-0522-4195-9e89-831ead04e19c"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Constraining High-energy Neutrino Emission from Supernovae with IceCube","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-720147","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: The relationship between CINV duration and recurrence in subsequent cycles is largely unstudied. Our objective was to determine if patients experiencing CINV in their first cycle of chemotherapy (C1) would face increased risk of CINV in later cycles and whether the duration of the CINV would predict increased risk of recurrence. PATIENTS AND METHODS: Using data from a previously reported phase III trial, we assessed patients' recurrence of breakthrough CINV after antiemetic prophylaxis for anthracycline+cyclophosphamide (AC) for breast cancer, comparing C1 short CINV vs. extended CINV as a secondary analysis. Complete response (CR) and CINV duration were primary and secondary endpoints, respectively. CR was considered prophylaxis success; lack of CR was considered treatment failure (TF). RESULTS: Among 402 female patients, 99 (24.6%) had TF in C1 (TF1). The remaining 303 patients (CR1) had ≥93% CR rates in each subsequent cycle, while the 99 patients with TF1 had TF rates of 49.8% for cycles 2-4 (P < .001). The 51 patients with extended TF (≥3 days) in C1 had recurrent TF in 73/105 later cycles (69.5%, P < .001), while the 48 patients with short TF (1-2 days) in C1 had recurrent TF in 33/108 later cycles (30.6%). The relative risk of recurrence after C1 extended TF was 2.28 (CI 1.67-3.11; P < .001) compared to short TF. CONCLUSIONS: Prophylaxis success in C1 led to >90% repeat success across cycles of AC-based chemotherapy. For patients with breakthrough CINV, extended duration strongly predicted recurrent CINV. The duration of CINV should be closely monitored, and augmenting antiemetic prophylaxis considered for future cycles when extended CINV occurs.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Navari, Rudolph","Binder, Gary","Molasiotis, Alex","Herrstedt, Jørn","Roeland, Eric J.","Ruddy, Kathryn J.","LeBlanc, Thomas W.","Kloth, Dwight D.","Klute, Kelsey A.","Papademetriou, Eros","Schmerold, Luke","Schwartzberg, Lee"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-732559"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Duke University","Fox Chase Cancer Center","Helsinn Therapeutics US Inc.","Mayo Clinic Rochester, MN","Oregon Health and Science University","Renown Regional Medical Center","SmartAnalyst, Inc.","University of Derby","University of Nebraska Medical Center","World Health Organization"],"keyword":["Antiemetic","Breast cancer","Chemotherapy","Nausea","Vomiting"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"duration of chemotherapy induced nausea and vomiting cinv as a predictor of recurrent cinv in later cycles","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/oncolo/oyac240","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-732559","identifier":[{"id":"PMC10020801","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"36527702","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85150666541","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-d8ed71d1-92a7-44a0-8175-da1d9ffc228b"],"keyword":["antiemetic","breast cancer","chemotherapy","nausea","vomiting"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-AEE261EEF124316C3B056B08AEDAADFB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/387272593/oyac240.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/oncolo/oyac240","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ch","key":"world health organization","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"World Health 