fno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Rainer","key":"freynhagenpau","last":"Freynhagen","name":"Freynhagen, Rainer","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeffrey D","key":"kennedypau","last":"Kennedy","name":"Kennedy, Jeffrey D","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Walter","key":"magerlpau","last":"Magerl","name":"Magerl, Walter","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Tina","key":"mainkapau","last":"Mainka","name":"Mainka, Tina","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Maren","key":"reimerpau","last":"Reimer","name":"Reimer, Maren","rank":16,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrew S C","key":"ricepau","last":"Rice","name":"Rice, Andrew S C","rank":17,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Märta","key":"segerdahlpau","last":"Segerdahl","name":"Segerdahl, Märta","rank":18,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jordi","key":"serrapau","last":"Serra","name":"Serra, Jordi","rank":19,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Sören","key":"sindruppau","last":"Sindrup","locid":"322d2520-9f89-49bd-92b3-565f53ffddcd","name":"Sindrup, Søren Hein","name_org":"Sindrup, Sören","orcid":"0000-0003-0357-8628","rank":20,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Claudia","key":"sommerpau","last":"Sommer","name":"Sommer, Claudia","rank":21,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"töllepau","last":"Tölle","name":"Tölle, Thomas","rank":22,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jan","key":"vollertpau","last":"Vollert","name":"Vollert, Jan","rank":23,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Rolf-Detlef","key":"treedepau","last":"Treede","name":"Treede, Rolf-Detlef","rank":24,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["27893485"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1097/j.pain.0000000000000753","issn":["03043959","18726623"],"issn_org":["03043959"],"issue":"2","journal":"Pain","pages":"261-272","publisher":"Lippincott Williams and Wilkins","status":"p","title":"Pain","volume":"158"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85018196890"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Lundbeck Pharma","Private Research","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-8358ebe7-ee26-4531-913c-f2b4b1d2956f","sdu-da4cc53c-2a30-4c1f-beae-992f81bd6dda"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Peripheral Neuropathic Pain: A mechanism-related organizing principle based on sensory profiles","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.35-022254","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Koskinen et al. (2011) have proposed a framework for what they call constructive design research that more consistently than the broad notions of research through design (Fraylin 1993) or research through practice (Archer 1995) adhere to particular methodologies that produce knowledge through prototyping new possibilities. Thus the nexus of constructive design (research) is exploring the possible through making. Schön (1983) early argued that design practices are based on different kinds of experimentation, and that this opens up a space of virtuality in which the questions of whatever what is imagined can be done, where the experiment leads us and how we appreciate the outcome are raised simultaneously. For Schön this leads to a conceptualization of the designer as a reflective practitioner, who in an on-going dialogue with what he calls ‘the materials of the design situation’, establish both problem and solution through iterations of naming and framing, re-naming and re-framing the situation. Designers typically work from a (design) brief or a program that sets goals for what is to be achieved by the design The program operates as the first framing of a design space within which to explore possibilities through experimentation (Brandt and Binder 2007). Likewise in relation to design research we have argued that “the possible is always contingent and though research may convincingly provide arguments for certain possibilities both search and arguments have to be guided by programs that set a direction” (Binder et al. 2011). Along these lines we see the program as a provisional knowledge regime in the sense “that it is not unquestionably presupposed but rather functions as a sort of hypothetical worldview that makes the particular inquiry relevant. As the design work unfolds, it will either substantiate or challenge this view and the dialectic between program and probing is in our view central to any design practice” (Binder and Redström 2006: 4). Our suggestion is to see design research practices as fundamentally homologous to any other design practices both in terms of the way they are driven forward by a dialectic between program and experiment and in how they actualize potentialities through experientially manifesting ‘the possible’. This does not mean that design practices are in themselves research practices. Research practices must be answerable to a research question or concern that resides outside the program. Still exploring the possible can only be pursued through adhering to a program (Brandt and Binder 2007). Figure 1 shows our early attempts to visualize the relationship between overall research question, design research program and experiments in various ways. The main diagram to the left illustrates that the overall research question is larger than the actual research project, in the sense that different research groups often focus on similar issues and questions but apply e.g. various research methodologies, relate to various contexts etc. to investigate the research matter.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Binder, Thomas","Brandt, Eva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-128153"],"filters":{"added":"2023-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Experimental design research","Practice Based Research","collaborative encounters"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-ART"],"level3":{"ART":["DI-ART-ALL","DI-ART-RDKA"]},"level3all":["DI-ART-RDKA"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"design research practice","src":["arkc"],"src-code":["DI-ART-RDKA"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85018942631","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-128153","identifier":[{"id":"85018942631","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["arkc-c54a0221-91a9-4018-8323-ccdf387f6695"],"keyword":["No","collaborative encounters","design(research) program","experimental design research","practice based research"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-6A096213D2363D3C25C7E6467B7B2677"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"royal danish academy architecture design conservation","l2":"DI-ART","l2n":"Artistic Higher Education Institutions","l3":"DI-ART-RDKA","l3nn":"Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation","namevar":["Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation, Royal Danish Academy - Design, Institute of Design and Visual Interaction","Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation, Royal Danish Academy - Design, Institute of Design and Visual Interaction, CODE - Centre for Codesign","The Royal Danish Academy of Fine Arts, Schools of Architecture, Design and Conservation","The Royal Danish Academy of Fine Arts, Schools of Architecture, Design and Conservation, School of Design"],"nora_code":"DI-ART-RDKA","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Thomas","key":"binderpau","last":"Binder","locid":"c74da6b3-52d3-4ac5-9078-0921bcae572c","name":"Binder, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0001-8244-1798","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Eva","key":"brandtpau","last":"Brandt","locid":"4186dd98-15a8-4d67-a630-5d0875e85920","name":"Brandt, Eva","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Laurene","key":"vaughanpedh","last":"Vaughan","name":"Vaughan, Laurene","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9781474267809"],"pages":"101-110","place":"Bloomsbury Academic","status":"p","title":"Practice Based Design Research"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85055912537"],"search":{"DAF":["All Artistic Higher Education Institutions","All Danish Affiliations","Artistic Higher Education Institutions","DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-ART-ALL"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["arkc-c54a0221-91a9-4018-8323-ccdf387f6695"],"src":["Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation"],"title":"Design (research) practice","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-128153","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"1. Electronic telemetry is frequently used to document animal movement through time. Methods that can identify underlying behaviors driving specific movement patterns can help us understand how and why animals use available space, thereby aiding conservation and management efforts. For aquatic animal tracking data with significant measurement error, a Bayesian state-space model called the first-Difference Correlated Random Walk with Switching (DCRWS) has often been used for this purpose. However, for aquatic animals, highly accurate tracking data of animal movement are now becoming more common. 2. We developed a new Hidden Markov Model (HMM) for identifying behavioral states from animal tracks with negligible error, which we called the Hidden Markov Movement Model (HMMM). We implemented as the basis for the HMMM the process equation of the DCRWS, but we used the method of maximum likelihood and the R package TMB for rapid model fitting. 3. We compared the HMMM to a modified version of the DCRWS for highly accurate tracks, the DCRWSnome, and to a common HMM for animal tracks fitted with the R package moveHMM. We show that the HMMM is both accurate and suitable for multiple species by fitting it to real tracks from a grey seal, lake trout, and blue shark, as well as to simulated data. 4. The HMMM is a fast and reliable tool for making meaningful inference from animal movement data that is ideally suited for ecologists who want to use the popular DCRWS implementation for highly accurate tracking data. It additionally provides a groundwork for development of more complex modelling of animal movement with TMB. To facilitate its uptake, we make it available through the R package swim.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Whoriskey, Kim","Auger-Méthé, Marie","Albertsen, Christoffer Moesgaard","Whoriskey, Frederick G.","Binder, Thomas R.","Krueger, Charles C.","Flemming, Joanna Mills"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-376530"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["Canada","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Dalhousie University","Michigan State University"],"keyword":["q bio QM"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a hidden markov movement model for rapidly identifying behavioral states from animal tracks","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.2795","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-376530","identifier":[{"id":"PMC5383489","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85014103753","type":"scopus"},{"id":"28405277","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-f06bcd22-392c-4b80-8ba5-0c1db8eb4bf3"],"keyword":["q-bio.QM"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-90E396ECCAA70905599648065DB6112A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.2795","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/129907962/Publishers_version.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ca","key":"dalhousie university","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Dalhousie University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, National Institute of Aquatic Resources","Technical University of Denmark, National Institute of Aquatic Resources, Section for Marine Living Resources"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"michigan state university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Michigan State University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kim","key":"whoriskeykimpau","last":"Whoriskey","name":"Whoriskey, Kim","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marie","key":"augerméthémariepau","last":"Auger-Méthé","name":"Auger-Méthé, Marie","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Christoffer Moesgaard","key":"albertsenchristoffermoesgaardpau","last":"Albertsen","locid":"08bc041a-1750-49eb-ba12-4bcd485fa8d7","name":"Albertsen, Christoffer Moesgaard","orcid":"0000-0003-0088-4363","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Frederick G.","key":"whoriskeyfrederickgpau","last":"Whoriskey","name":"Whoriskey, Frederick G.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas R.","key":"binderthomasrpau","last":"Binder","name":"Binder, Thomas R.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Charles C.","key":"kruegercharlescpau","last":"Krueger","name":"Krueger, Charles C.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Joanna Mills","key":"flemmingjoannamillspau","last":"Flemming","name":"Flemming, Joanna Mills","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["28405277"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/ece3.2795","issn":["20457758"],"issue":"7","journal":"Ecology and Evolution","pages":"2112-2121","publisher":"John Wiley & Sons Ltd","status":"p","title":"Ecology and Evolution","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85014103753"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-f06bcd22-392c-4b80-8ba5-0c1db8eb4bf3"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"A Hidden Markov Movement Model for rapidly identifying behavioral states from animal tracks","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-376530","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Exfoliation syndrome (XFS) is the most common known risk factor for secondary glaucoma and a major cause of blindness worldwide. Variants in two genes, LOXL1 and CACNA1A, have previously been associated with XFS. To further elucidate the genetic basis of XFS, we collected a global sample of XFS cases to refine the association at LOXL1, which previously showed inconsistent results across populations, and to identify new variants associated with XFS. We identified a rare protective allele at LOXL1 (p.Phe407, odds ratio (OR) = 25, P = 2.9 × 10−14) through deep resequencing of XFS cases and controls from nine countries. A genome-wide association study (GWAS) of XFS cases and controls from 24 countries followed by replication in 18 countries identified seven genome-wide significant loci (P < 5 × 10−8). We identified association signals at 13q12 (POMP), 11q23.3 (TMEM136), 6p21 (AGPAT1), 3p24 (RBMS3) and 5q23 (near SEMA6A). These findings provide biological insights into the pathology of XFS and highlight a potential role for naturally occurring rare LOXL1 variants in disease biology.","author_count":289,"contributors":["Aung, Tin","Ozaki, Mineo","Lee, Mei Chin","Schlotzer-Schrehardt, Ursula","Thorleifsson, Gudmar","Mizoguchi, Takanori","Igo, Robert P., Jr.","Haripriya, Aravind","Williams, Susan E.","Astakhov, Yury S.","Orr, Andrew C.","Burdon, Kathryn P.","Nakano, Satoko","Mori, Kazuhiko","Abu-Amero, Khaled","Hauser, Michael","Li, Zheng","Prakadeeswari, Gopalakrishnan","Bailey, Jessica N. Cooke","Cherecheanu, Alina Popa","Kang, Jae H.","Nelson, Sarah","Hayashi, Ken","Manabe, Shin-ichi","Kazama, Shigeyasu","Zarnowski, Tomasz","Inoue, Kenji","Irkec, Murat","Coca-Prados, Miguel","Sugiyama, Kazuhisa","Jarvela, Irma","Schlottmann, Patricio","Lerner, S. Fabian","Lamari, Hasnaa","Nilgun, Yildirim","Bikbov, Mukharram","Park, Ki Ho","Cha, Soon Cheol","Yamashiro, Kenji","Zenteno, Juan C.","Jonas, Jost B.","Kumar, Rajesh S.","Perera, Shamira A.","Chan, Anita S. Y.","Kobakhidze, Nino","George, Ronnie","Vijaya, Lingam","Do, Tan","Edward, Deepak P.","de Juan Marcos, Lourdes","Pakravan, Mohammad","Moghimi, Sasan","Ideta, Ryuichi","Bach-Holm, Daniella","Kappelgaard, Per","Wirostko, Barbara","Thomas, Samuel","Gaston, Daniel","Bedard, Karen","Greer, Wenda L.","Yang, Zhenglin","Chen, Xueyi","Huang, Lulin","Sang, Jinghong","Jia, Hongyan","Jia, Liyun","Qiao, Chunyan","Zhang, Hui","Liu, Xuyang","Zhao, Bowen","Wang, Ya-Xing","Xu, Liang","Leruez, Stephanie","Reynier, Pascal","Chichua, George","Tabagari, Sergo","Uebe, Steffen","Zenkel, Matthias","Berner, Daniel","Mossboeck, Georg","Weisschuh, Nicole","Hoja, Ursula","Welge-Luessen, Ulrich-Christoph","Mardin, Christian","Founti, Panayiota","Chatzikyriakidou, Anthi","Pappas, Theofanis","Anastasopoulos, Eleftherios","Lambropoulos, Alexandros","Ghosh, Arkasubhra","Shetty, Rohit","Porporato, Natalia","Saravanan, Vijayan","Venkatesh, Rengaraj","Shivkumar, Chandrashekaran","Kalpana, Narendran","Sarangapani, Sripriya","Kanavi, Mozhgan R.","Beni, Afsaneh Naderi","Yazdani, Shahin","Lashay, Alireza","Naderifar, Homa","Khatibi, Nassim","Fea, Antonio","Lavia, Carlo","Dallorto, Laura","Rolle, Teresa","Frezzotti, Paolo","Paoli, Daniela","Salvi, Erika","Manunta, Paolo","Mori, Yosai","Miyata, Kazunori","Higashide, Tomomi","Chihara, Etsuo","Ishiko, Satoshi","Yoshida, Akitoshi","Yanagi, Masahide","Kiuchi, Yoshiaki","Ohashi, Tsutomu","Sakurai, Toshiya","Sugimoto, Takako","Chuman, Hideki","Aihara, Makoto","Inatani, Masaru","Miyake, Masahiro","Gotoh, Norimoto","Matsuda, Fumihiko","Yoshimura, Nagahisa","Ikeda, Yoko","Ueno, Morio","Sotozono, Chie","Jeoung, Jin Wook","Sagong, Min","Park, Kyu Hyung","Ahn, Jeeyun","Cruz-Aguilar, Marisa","Ezzouhairi, Sidi M.","Rafei, Abderrahman","Chong, Yaan Fun","Ng, Xiao Yu","Goh, Shuang Ru","Chen, Yueming","Yong, Victor H. K.","Khan, Muhammad Imran","Olawoye, Olusola O.","Ashaye, Adeyinka O.","Ugbede, Idakwo","Onakoya, Adeola","Kizor-Akaraiwe, Nkiru","Teekhasaenee, Chaiwat","Suwan, Yan