le":"en publikation om kunstneriske arbejdsprocesser","title":"Overskud"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Artistic Higher Education Institutions","All Danish Affiliations","Artistic Higher Education Institutions","DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-ART-ALL"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["arkc-803ec9e9-0e63-41b9-a934-10467f026d83"],"src":["Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation"],"title":"Bevægelse i (kunstens) laboratorier","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-127317","year":"2011","yearsub":"2011"}
{"abstract":"This book is a report from a class on design research at the Danish Design School. Ten students had ten weeks to conduct design research based on approaches, methods and techniques from co-design focusing on the Alzheimer’s disease as experienced by people with Alzheimer’s, their relatives and close relations, and professional caretakers. Alzheimer's destroys brain cells, causing memory loss and problems with thinking and behavior severe enough to affect work, lifelong hobbies and social life. As Alzheimer’s is a fatal disease that affects many people, there is a strong interest in finding ways to better understand the consequences of the disease, spread the knowledge to more people and diagnosis the disease as soon as possible. The class was partly sponsored by Johnson & Johnson, an international pharmaceutical company. The students also collaborated with the Danish Alzheimer Association. Future designers need competence in co-design. Just as important is the need to establish various formats for how to maintain the research results and make these a valuable asset for further inquiry. Thus, the outcome of the class is a box that can be used by Johnson & Johnson and other professional organizations in a user-driven approach to explore and learn what it means to identify and admit that a person has Alzheimer’s. Apart from making contributions to the common box, the students have written reports in which they reflect on their work both in relation to the design research process and the outcome. This book is an edited version of their reports written by the students. Our hope is that it can be an inspiration for other people. Thomas Binder, Eva Brandt and Morten Noer Andersen Lecturers, The Danish Design School","author_count":3,"contributors":["Brandt, Eva","Binder, Thomas","no-lastname, no-firstname"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-126229"],"filters":{"added":"2023-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Alzheimer s","Co design","Design research","Everyday life"],"level":"pop","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-ART"],"level3":{"ART":["DI-ART-ALL","DI-ART-RDKA"]},"level3all":["DI-ART-RDKA"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"design research six views in a box dialogues on everyday life with alzheimer s","src":["arkc"],"src-code":["DI-ART-RDKA"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-126229","ids":["arkc-1ff4767e-e12c-421a-9462-2dd41d1b88cf"],"keyword":["Alzheimer's","Co-design","Design research","Everyday life","No"],"lang":"English","level":"Popular","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-B100B4423EE1927BAAC7A4877B830E6D"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"royal danish academy architecture design conservation","l2":"DI-ART","l2n":"Artistic Higher Education Institutions","l3":"DI-ART-RDKA","l3nn":"Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation","namevar":["The Royal Danish Academy of Fine Arts, Schools of Architecture, Design and Conservation, School of Design","The Royal Danish Academy of Fine Arts, Schools of Architecture, Design and Conservation, School of Design, Danish Centre for Design Research, DKDS"],"nora_code":"DI-ART-RDKA","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Eva","key":"brandtped","last":"Brandt","locid":"4186dd98-15a8-4d67-a630-5d0875e85920","name":"Brandt, Eva","rank":1,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Thomas","key":"binderped","last":"Binder","locid":"c74da6b3-52d3-4ac5-9078-0921bcae572c","name":"Binder, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0001-8244-1798","rank":2,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"no-firstname","key":"nolastnamepau","last":"no-lastname","name":"no-lastname, no-firstname","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788792016219"],"pages":"103","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Artistic Higher Education Institutions","All Danish Affiliations","Artistic Higher Education Institutions","DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-ART-ALL"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["arkc-1ff4767e-e12c-421a-9462-2dd41d1b88cf"],"src":["Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation"],"title":"Design Research: Six Views in a Box : Dialogues on everyday life with Alzheimer's","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-126229","year":"2011","yearsub":"2011"}
{"abstract":"If height-limiting treatment is being considered for a child with tall stature, skeletal maturity is invaluable in the selection of appropriate patients for treatment, determining appropriate age of treatment commencement, monitoring progress of treatment, and determining the expected treatment effect on adult height. In precocious puberty, bone maturation can be usefully assessed at initial diagnosis and start of treatment and at regular intervals thereafter during treatment monitoring. Together with height, bone maturation is an essential parameter for long-term treatment monitoring in congenital adrenal hyperplasia. Bone age (BA) determination in children with skeletal dysplasia is only feasible in a few disorders and estimations should be treated with caution. Radiographs of the left hand and wrist are, however, essential in the diagnosis of many skeletal disorders. Bone mineralization and measures of bone lengths, width, thickness and cortical thickness should always be evaluated in relation to a child's height and BA, especially around puberty. The use of skeletal maturity, assessed on a radiograph alone to estimate chronological age for immigration authorities or criminal courts is not recommended.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Martin, D.D.","Binder, Gitte Sommer","Ranke, M.B.","Fricke, O.","Wudy, S.A.","Wit, Janneke","Van Rijn, R.R.","Hochberg, Z.","Werther, G.","Cameron, N.","Butler, G.","Hertel, Niels Thomas","Thodberg, Hans Henrik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-619117"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, Middle East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","Germany","Israel","Netherlands","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["Academic Medical Center","All Global Affiliations","Justus Liebig University Giessen","Loughborough University","Rambam Medical Center","The Royal Children's Hospital","University College London Hospitals NHS Fou